In TypeScript, both interfaces and types are used to define the structure of objects, but they serve slightly different purposes and have some key differences.

An interface in TypeScript is a blueprint for objects. It defines a contract that objects must adhere to, specifying the properties and methods that must be present. Interfaces are primarily used for type checking and can be implemented by classes to ensure they conform to the defined structure. For example:

```typescript
interface Person {
  name: string;
  age: number;
}

class Employee implements Person {
  name: string;
  age: number;

  constructor(name: string, age: number) {
    this.name = name;
    this.age = age;
  }
}
```

A type, on the other hand, is a more flexible concept. It can be a type alias, a mapped type, or a union type, among others. Types can be used to create more complex type definitions and are not limited to object structures. For instance, you can create a type alias for a specific type:

```typescript
type Person = {
  name: string;
  age: number;
};

type Employee = Person & {
  department: string;
};
```

In summary, while interfaces are used to define object structures and are implemented by classes, types offer more flexibility and can be used for various type-related tasks, including creating aliases, mapped types, and union types. Both are powerful tools in TypeScript for ensuring type safety and enhancing code readability and maintainability.

